1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
So, you've got four ways to extend Claude, which is great, until you're staring at a

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:07,000
real feature and you have to pick one.

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:11,000
Reach for the wrong mechanism, and you either can't do the job, or you overbuild it.

4
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:17,000
This lesson covers how to choose the right one, quickly and for the right reason.

5
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:20,000
Here's the guiding principle for the whole lesson.

6
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:23,000
Pick the lightest mechanism that actually does the job.

7
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:25,000
That word, lightest, matters.

8
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:28,000
The common mistake is reaching for something heavy.

9
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:34,000
Opening up an MCP server, or writing a skill, when a simple built-in or custom tool would

10
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:36,000
have covered it.

11
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:39,000
Every mechanism you add carries cost.

12
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:41,000
Tool definitions consume context.

13
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:45,000
A server is infrastructure you now own and maintain.

14
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:48,000
So the instinct should run the other way.

15
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:53,000
Start from the simplest thing that fits and only move up when you have a concrete reason.

16
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:56,000
If a built-in tool covers the need, you're done.

17
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:59,000
If not, a custom tool.

18
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:05,000
Only when the requirement outgrows those do you reach for a skill or an MCP server.

19
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:10,000
Let me give you three questions that resolve most decisions. First question.

20
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:13,000
Does an existing built-in tool already cover this?

21
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:20,000
If Anthropic hosts the capability, like web search or running code, use it and stop there.

22
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:21,000
Second question.

23
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:25,000
Is this one specific action for a single application?

24
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:31,000
If so, a custom tool you define and run is the right size. Third question.

25
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:37,000
Does this capability need to be reused, shared across many clients, teams, or projects?

26
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:40,000
That's when a skill or an MCP server earns its weight.

27
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:45,000
A skill if what you're reusing is procedure and know-how.

28
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:48,000
An MCP server if it's access to a system.

29
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:51,000
Three questions and they cascade.

30
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:53,000
Built-in first, then custom.

31
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:57,000
And only then the heavier, reusable options.

32
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:01,000
Most of the time you'll have your answer by the second question.

33
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:05,000
Let's take the branches one at a time, starting with built-in tools.

34
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:10,000
Reach for these first whenever they cover the need because they cost you the least.

35
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:15,000
There's no handler to write, no server to run, and no integration to maintain.

36
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:18,000
You enable the tool and Claude can use it.

37
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:26,000
Web search, web fetch, code execution, and tool search handle a surprising range of common needs.

38
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:31,000
Pulling in public information, running a calculation, working with fetched content.

39
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:34,000
The one thing to remember is they're sealing.

40
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:39,000
They run on Anthropic's infrastructure, so they stop at your firewall.

41
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:46,000
The moment the job requires your private data or an internal service, a built-in tool can't get there.

42
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:49,000
Then you move to the next option.

43
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:53,000
When a built-in can't reach far enough and the need is specific to your application,

44
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:57,000
a custom tool is usually the right call.

45
00:02:57,000 --> 00:02:58,000
This is the workhorse.

46
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:01,000
You're giving Claude one well-defined action.

47
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:06,000
Look up this customer, create this record, hit this internal endpoint, and it lives inside

48
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:08,000
the app you're building.

49
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:13,000
You write the description and schema, you run the handler, and you're connected to whatever

50
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:15,000
your code can reach.

51
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:17,000
Notice we haven't left simple territory yet.

52
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:23,000
A custom tool is more work than flipping on a built-in, but far less than standing up

53
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:25,000
a server or authoring a skill.

54
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:31,000
So if the capability belongs to this one application and nobody else needs it, stop here.

55
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:36,000
Don't reach for MCP just because it sounds more robust.

56
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:40,000
Now the pair people most often confuse, skills and MCP.

57
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:45,000
The mistake is treating them as rivals, as if you choose one instead of the other.

58
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:46,000
They solve different problems.

59
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:49,000
A skill packages procedural knowledge.

60
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:55,000
It teaches Claude how to carry out a workflow, the steps and conventions, done the same way every time.

61
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:57,000
MCP is about connectivity.

62
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:03,000
It's the standardized wiring that gives Claude access to external tools, resources, and data.

63
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:06,000
One is know-how, the other is reach.

64
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:10,000
So the honest answer is often not skill versus MCP at all.

65
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:15,000
You use a skill to encode the procedure and an MCP server to provide the systems that

66
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:18,000
procedure operates on.

67
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:22,000
Let me build on that because the combination is where it clicks.

68
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:25,000
Anthropic's own framing is explicit.

69
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:31,000
Skills can complement MCP servers by teaching Claude the more complex workflows that involve

70
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:32,000
those external tools.

71
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:38,000
Picture an MCP server that exposes a set of actions against some system.

72
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:44,000
On its own, Claude has the tools, but not your team's particular way of using them.

73
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:49,000
Add a skill, and now Claude knows the procedure, which tool to call in what order, what conventions

74
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:52,000
to follow, how to handle the edge cases.

75
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:55,000
The MCP server brings the reach.

76
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:58,000
The skill brings the method.

77
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:05,000
And skills stay cheap on context through progressive disclosure, so pairing them doesn't bloat every request.

78
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:08,000
Capability plus know-how, working together.

79
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:13,000
Step back and the whole decision comes down to the three forces you met last lesson.

80
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:17,000
Reach, reuse scope, and context cost.

81
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:21,000
Reach rules out built-in tools when the capability has to touch systems.

82
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:23,000
Anthropic doesn't host.

83
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:29,000
Reuse scope splits custom tools, one app, from MCP servers shared across clients and

84
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:34,000
teams with shared procedure pointing to a skill.

85
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:39,000
And context cost is why the heavier mechanisms are never the default.

86
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:40,000
Try a quick example.

87
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:45,000
You need Claude to file tickets in an internal tracker for one team.

88
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:46,000
Reach rules out built-ins.

89
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:50,000
The tracker sits behind your firewall.

90
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:55,000
Reuse says single app, so a custom tool beats an MCP server.

91
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:56,000
Context cost agrees.

92
00:05:56,000 --> 00:06:00,000
One small schema instead of a server catalog.

93
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:05,000
The way those three, and the right mechanism, usually names itself.

94
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:10,000
One last idea before we wrap, and it frees you from thinking you have to pick just one.

95
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:14,000
A real agent often uses several of these at once.

96
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:20,000
Claude might use a built-in tool for web search, a custom tool for one internal action, an

97
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:25,000
MCP server for a shared system, and a skill that ties the workflow together, all in the

98
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:27,000
same application.

99
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:29,000
They aren't mutually exclusive.

100
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:34,000
The decision framework isn't about choosing a single winner for the whole agent.

101
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:36,000
It's applied per capability.

102
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:42,000
For each thing you want Claude to do, you ask which mechanism is the lightest fit, and

103
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:46,000
you may well land on different answers for different capabilities.

104
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:51,000
Mix them deliberately, and each does the job it's best suited for.

105
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:53,000
Here's the bottom line.

106
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:56,000
Choosing among these four comes down to one habit.

107
00:06:56,000 --> 00:06:59,000
Search for the lightest mechanism that fits.

108
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:06,000
Built-in when Anthropic hosts it, a custom tool for one app's action, a skill for reusable

109
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:10,000
procedure, MCP when a capability is shared.

110
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:14,000
And remember, skills and MCP complement each other.

111
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:16,000
They don't compete.

112
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:18,000
Match each need to its mechanism.

